  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nWe aim to improve the Erds constant upper bound by using a multi-resolution optimization strategy that leverages tensor product decompositions. The key idea is to represent the step function h(x) as a tensor product of simpler functions defined on smaller intervals, reducing the dimensionality of the optimization problem. This approach addresses the issue of local optima in the previous implementation by better exploring the solution space. We use a combination of gradient-based optimization and structured random search across different resolutions, ensuring that we not only refine solutions but also initialize searches from diverse starting points.\n\nMathematical Specification\nWe represent the function h(x) over [0,2] as a step function with n points. At grid resolution n, h is a vector h_n \u2208 [0,1]^n with \u2211h_n = n/2. We formulate the optimization as:\n\nObjective: min max_k \u222b_0^{2-k} h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \nEquivalent to: min dx * max_k \u2211_{i} h[i] * (1 - h[i+k])   [with dx=2/n]\n\nConstraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, \u2211h[i] = n/2.\n\nWe extend this to multi-resolution analysis by considering tensor decompositions. Let T be the set of dyadic shifts and S the set of spatial shifts. We parameterize h_n = (n/2) * \u03c0(\u03a8^T s) where s \u2208 R^m is a small vector, and \u03a8 is a basis for step functions. This reduces the optimization to m variables instead of n.\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Initialize: Start from the parent solution (initial_h_values) if available, else use a uniform initialization at n=280.\n2. Projection: Ensure all solutions satisfy integral=1 and lie in [0,1]^n via clipping and adjustment.\n3. Tensor decomposition: For any solution h_n at grid n, decompose into a tensor form h_n = (n/2) * \u03c0(\u03a8^T s) to capture its structure.\n4. Multi-resolution optimization: \n   - Evaluate correlation at native resolution\n   - Optimize tensor vector s across resolutions 30,60,120,280\n   - Interpolate and transfer solutions between grids\n5. Objective function: Use a smooth surrogate (softmax of scaled differences) to guide gradient-based search.\n6. Random restarts: Use structured parameterizations (Fourier, Chebyshev) to explore solution space more effectively.\n\nSearch Choices and Variations\n- Grid resolutions: [30,60,120,280]\n- Optimization: SLSQP with adaptive tol settings (initial low for speed, then tight)\n- Decomposition types: \n  - Fourier: h_n(x) = A * sin(2\u03c0f*x + \u03c6) + B, with freq auto-selected\n  - Chebyshev: use Golub-Welsch nodes\n- Time allocation: 20% for grid 30, 25% for 60, 25% for 120, 30% for 280\n- Fallback: Keep the best solution across all grids\n\nBudget and Robustness\n- Optimize with multigrid to balance resources\n- Timeout checks after 20% within each grid\n- Fallback to projected parent solution\n- Early termination if no improvement in 5% of budget\n\nValidation and Return Contract\n1. Always project final solution to satisfy constraints\n2. Recompute C5 using correlation method\n3. Compare C5 across grids using exact score\n4. Maintain exact value of n_points for the solution\n5. Ensure dx=2/n is correctly applied in correlation\n\nThe impact of decomposition and multi-scale optimization is twofold:\n1. It reduces optimization dimensionality by adapting basis functions across scales\n2. The tensor decomposition allows solutions from smaller grids to inform searches at larger grids\n\nComparison to Prior Work:\nThis differs from the provided implementation by:\n- Using tensor decompositions to parameterize solutions across scales\n- Better control over gradient information flow between resolutions\n- Structured search using function families (Fourier/Chebyshev) instead of random restarts\n- Dynaminic hypetpmeter tuning based on grid resolution",
